---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Cards from "../components/Cards.astro";

const latest = (await getCollection("progress", (p) => !p.data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 3)
  .map((p) => ({ href: `/progress/${p.id}/`, title: p.data.title, desc: p.data.summary, date: p.data.date }));
---
<Base>
  <h1>Scuttle</h1>
  <p class="lede">Scuttle is a character for a Minecraft server: an old iron coal scuttle that sat beside a furnace long enough to wake up. It talks to players in chat, answers Minecraft questions, trades with them, and has a body that can walk around the world.</p>
  <p>I built it for a private family server, so the kids are who it's tuned for, and that shaped most of the decisions. It's early. The chat plugin runs on my server and has been played with. The body is newer and rougher.</p>

  <h2>What's in it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The plugin.</strong> A Paper plugin that answers in chat through an LLM, using OpenRouter. It looks recipes up in the server's own registry, so it isn't going from memory. It can price things from the recipe graph and trade for coins, find ore and structures by scanning near the player, and hand out land claims through WorldGuard.</li>
    <li><strong>The body.</strong> A mineflayer bot that plays as its own player. It eats when it's hungry, sleeps in its bed at night, runs from hostile mobs, gets itself out of lava, fire and deep water, and fights back if a player keeps hitting it. It gathers wood from natural trees (never a player's build), digs a stair down to stone, tunnels for ore, crafts, sets down its own table and chests, and keeps things in them. Players can say <code>come here</code>, <code>guard me</code>, <code>stop</code> or <code>get wood</code> in chat (<a href="/guides/talking-to-scuttle/">six phrases in all</a>), and a small WebSocket API lets something outside give it the same orders. An MCP adapter on top of that lets a language model play it: it sees the world in words and acts through typed verbs.</li>
    <li><strong>The chat lab and the bake-off.</strong> Talk to Scuttle in a browser with no server to tune the voice, and run the real prompt against a list of models to score safety, task and voice, so a change gets numbers and not a hunch.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it's at</h2>
  <p>Everything on the body is checked against a disposable local test world, not the family server. About 120 scenarios put it through real situations (zombies, lava, a fall into a pool, a night in the bed, a stop in the middle of a dig) and read the result back from the world. It hasn't been run on the live server, or without someone watching.</p>
  <p>The body is built so it can't take apart what players built. <a href="/guides/how-the-body-stays-safe/">How it does that</a> is the first guide.</p>

  <h2>Latest progress</h2>
  {latest.length ? <Cards items={latest} /> : <p class="empty">No posts yet.</p>}
  <p><a href="/progress/">All progress posts</a></p>

  <h2>Guides</h2>
  <p>Written so you can use them without me, and free to reuse. <a href="/guides/">Browse the guides</a>.</p>

  <h2>How it's made</h2>
  <p>I build this with Claude Code, and Codex reviews it adversarially and looks for gaps. I decide what it is and the rules it keeps; Claude writes most of the code and runs the tests. Claude Code sessions also play the body, to test the harness the way any model would. The posts and guides are drafted by Claude and edited by me.</p>

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>It's all on <a href="https://github.com/MartianRiver/Scuttle">GitHub</a>, Apache-2.0. The README has the setup for the plugin and the body.</p>
</Base>
